<p>Forty years after the 1986 Chornobyl disaster, reporting from the site shows that Ukraine is still managing serious nuclear safety risks as the war with Russia continues. The latest concern comes from damage in February 2025, when a Russian drone struck the New Safe Confinement built over the destroyed reactor, renewing fears that a place long defined by one catastrophe remains vulnerable to another. The coverage also tells a more hopeful parallel story: over decades, Ukrainian engineers and workers had reduced danger at the site through containment, monitoring, and cleanup before the full-scale invasion disrupted that progress. As the 40th anniversary approaches, Chornobyl stands both as a warning about the long life of nuclear contamination and as proof of sustained technical work to keep a damaged reactor under tighter control, even in wartime.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/l0peKJJ37W-wKOAbqAWShmwkuVgCZ457YcIKb27eiPWkv9AfKwxNDLV1l-F6YcPyYbxwdKaWLh21q6-b9JsiO4au0zNfg9r2T2ZRNehBjZyPGp0SYgJ71zfnTGdREf8-HIhpSaMYuZ7h8hT6N6kNYJRtzSxpOQjX4bioShFSiw' alt='Image from ABC&#x27;s report on wartime risks at the Chornobyl nuclear plant.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Shelter scale: The New Safe Confinement is described as the largest movable steel structure ever built, taller than the Statue of Liberty and wider than the Colosseum.</li><li>Invisible contamination: Inside the plant, workers and visitors still rely on dosimeters because radiation levels rise quickly when they step off designated paths, showing that contamination remains unevenly distributed across the site.</li><li>Legacy secrecy: Soviet authorities did not immediately disclose the full scale of the 1986 disaster, which spread radioactive fallout over what are now Ukraine and Belarus.</li><li>Second explosion: ABC frames the story around two explosions decades apart at the same plant, underscoring how a historic nuclear accident and a wartime strike now shape the site&#x27;s risk profile together.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Chornobyl workers: Workers told reporters the site is still not safe, even after years of stabilization work, because wartime damage has revived concerns about the confinement system and contamination control. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/news/ng-interactive/2026/apr/25/chornobyl-power-plant-at-risk-amid-russia-war-ukraine'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Ukrainian managers and engineers: Their view, reflected in the coverage, is that decades of engineering had meaningfully reduced risk at Chornobyl before Russia&#x27;s invasion disrupted operations and introduced new military threats to the facility. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/ukrainians-thought-reduced-risks-chernobyl-russia-invaded-132368484'>ABC News</a>)</li><li>Journalists revisiting the zone: PBS presents Chornobyl as both a historic symbol of Soviet mismanagement and an active site of cleanup and monitoring, not simply a closed chapter of Cold War history. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/inside-chernobyl-40-years-after-the-worlds-worst-nuclear-disaster'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/news/ng-interactive/2026/apr/25/chornobyl-power-plant-at-risk-amid-russia-war-ukraine'>Inside Chornobyl: 40 years after disaster, nuclear site still at risk in Russia’s war</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/ukrainians-thought-they-had-reduced-risks-at-chernobyl-then-russia-invaded-11406277#publisher=newsstand'>Ukrainians Thought They Had Reduced Risks At Chernobyl. Then Russia Invaded</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/ukrainians-thought-reduced-risks-chernobyl-russia-invaded-132368484'>Ukrainians thought they had reduced the risks at Chernobyl. Then Russia invaded</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/inside-chernobyl-40-years-after-the-worlds-worst-nuclear-disaster'>Inside Chornobyl, 40 years after the world&#x27;s worst nuclear disaster</a> - pbs.org</li></ul>